# Git hooks are run by `sh`. A CRLF here makes the shebang line read
# `#!/bin/sh\r`, which fails with a bare "no such file or directory" that
# names nothing — keep them LF on every platform.
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# THE MIRRORED SET IS PINNED TO LF (R08, 2026-08-17).
#
# `core.autocrlf` is true here, so the working tree is CRLF while git stores
# LF. The Drive mirror is written by copying the WORKING TREE, so a mirror copy
# produced by anything that writes LF — a non-Windows checkout, a different
# tool, an editor that normalizes on save — could never again compare equal to
# the CRLF copy beside it. The comparison in scripts/handoff.mjs now normalizes
# line endings, which fixes the symptom; this fixes the cause, so the two sides
# agree by construction rather than by both remembering to normalize.
#
# The measured case: CLAUDE.md is 45417 bytes in the working tree and 45147 in
# git, and the 270-byte gap is exactly its 270 CRLF line endings.
#
# This list is the text half of MIRRORED_DOCS in scripts/mirror-set.mjs. That
# module stays authoritative for WHAT is mirrored; this only fixes HOW those
# files are stored on disk. A file added there should be added here too.
CLAUDE.md            text eol=lf
docs/abuse-policy.md text eol=lf
docs/vendor-review.md text eol=lf
docs/source-ladder.md text eol=lf
docs/defects.md      text eol=lf
docs/state.md        text eol=lf
docs/tracker.csv     text eol=lf
docs/deferred.md     text eol=lf
docs/deferred.csv    text eol=lf
lib/sources/types.ts text eol=lf

# The workbook is the one binary in the mirrored set. Marking it explicitly
# keeps any future `text` wildcard from ever applying newline translation to
# it, which would corrupt the file rather than merely mis-compare it.
docs/deferred.xlsx   binary
